<genxml unit="mpc8xx" prefix="M8X" title="start-up values of the PowerPC communications controller on the L2 board">
  <doc>Start-up values of the PowerPC communications controller on the L2 board: the values the L2
reset code and its second stage write to the machine state register, the clock and reset
registers, the serial-management channel parameter page and the PCI bridge. Register offsets are
from the internal memory map base. The register and field names are the public Linux headers';
the firmware's own reading of each field was not decoded, so the layouts of the clock registers
and the bridge are not given. A value is established when the image writes it as stated.
</doc>
  <space name="bytedev" unit="byte"/>
  <space name="mpc8xx_brg" unit="byte"/>
  <space name="mpc8xx_clk" unit="byte"/>
  <space name="mpc8xx_cs" unit="byte"/>
  <space name="mpc8xx_pcib" unit="byte"/>
  <space name="mpc8xx_siu" unit="byte"/>
  <space name="mpc8xx_upm" unit="byte"/>
  <struct name="REG0" size="1" grade="established" register="yes" offset="0x0" access="w" space="bytedev" reset="unknown">
    <doc>Byte-wide peripheral on the fourth chip select. Established: the window decode above, the
8-bit port, and that the boot code only writes it, in the order given. What the device is
stays unknown, so the three registers are named by offset. Whether the kernel or any
program uses the part after mapping the first 4 KiB is not settled.</doc>
  </struct>
  <struct name="REG1" size="1" grade="established" register="yes" offset="0x1" access="w" space="bytedev" reset="unknown">
  </struct>
  <struct name="REG3" size="1" grade="established" register="yes" offset="0x3" access="w" space="bytedev" reset="unknown">
  </struct>
  <struct name="BRGC1" size="4" grade="inferred" register="yes" offset="0x9f0" access="rw" space="mpc8xx_brg" reset="unknown">
    <doc>First baud-rate generator, loaded by stage A of the console loader. Its place in the register
map is the public map's: the store goes through a base that sits 0x30 below the register, and
the same routine uses that base for the communications-processor command register and for
port B (read in l2.bin 1.44.0), so the position is inferred. Stage A computes the divisor
field as (clock word &gt;&gt; 4) / baud, doubled, with the enable bit or-ed in; with its clock word
of 0x32 and baud word 0x9600 the quotient is zero, so it stores only the enable bit. The
wrapper's clock word is 50,000,000, which gives the quotient 81 and the value 0x100a2.</doc>
  </struct>
  <struct name="SCCR" size="4" grade="established" register="yes" offset="0x280" access="rw" space="mpc8xx_clk" reset="unknown">
    <doc>Clock and reset registers written by the reset code; the multiplication factor and
